
### ④ `.gitignore` — make `git add .` safe so a stray test can't wipe your evidence
Right now `run_report.json` is *tracked*, so any later `python main.py …` overwrites it with scratch content, and a `git add .` would commit a regression over your 100‑row proof (and sweep in `results.*` junk). Full overwrite of `.gitignore` (small, safe) fixes it by tracking only the frozen `smoke-*` evidence and ignoring live scratch:

```gitignore
# secrets & local state (never submit)
.env
*.local
signalpost.db
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.DS_Store
.streamlit/secrets.toml

# live scratch outputs (regenerated per run; the FROZEN evidence is smoke-*)
results.json
results.csv
results.jsonl
run_report.json

# keep committed: smoke100.* , smoke-results.* , smoke-run-report.json , judge_keys.py , .env.example